Up to 4% of people in the U.S. live with bipolar disorder, what used to be called manic depression. But as common as this mood disorder is, it is also often misunderstood and misdiagnosed. Indeed, many people with bipolar disorder wait years between experiencing their first symptoms and receiving an accurate diagnosis. And the general public perception that bipolar disorder is always severe and untreatable is both harmful and out of date. For the past few decades, researchers have made great strides in understanding how to recognize, diagnose, and treat bipolar disorder with both psychotherapy and medications.
