抑郁症。但正如特里所暗示的，抗抑郁药的问题在于它们会增加循环。它们会导致更快速的循环，从高涨到低落的转换更频繁。然后是心理疗法。我们在实验室中测试的特定疗法是以家庭为中心的治疗，侧重于家庭内部的功能运作，让家庭了解什么是双相情感障碍、如何就此进行沟通、如何在不接管患者生活的情况下给予支持、如何在不具有控制欲的情况下鼓励依从服药、如何在病情发展时识别早期预警信号。所有这些，家庭都可以在这个过程中提供相当大的帮助。其他人更愿意走个人路线，找一个独立治疗师，在患有这种疾病的情况下帮助他们建立自我认同，或者找一个认知行为治疗师，与他们一起处理他们的自我对话或归因。我认为小组对于双相情感障碍患者非常有用，特别是如果其中包含其他也有这种疾病的人，这可以为他们提供关于如何应对的建议。所以最有效的治疗方法是药物和心理疗法的结合。特里，这也是你正在做的吗？你是在进行两者的结合吗？而且我知道你也写过关于在很长一段时间内服用大量不同药物的“鸡尾酒”疗法的文章，你如何应对这种情况？

22
00:14:21.000 --> 00:15:05.000
them kind of with their identity formation despite having this disorder or a cognitive behavioral therapists who might work with them on their self-talk or their attributions. Groups, I think, are very useful for people with bipolar disorder, especially if they involve other people who have the disorder and it can give them advice on how to cope. So most effective treatments are a combination of medications and psychotherapy. And is that what you're doing, Terry? is are you doing a combination of both? And I know you've also written about sort of taking a cocktail of a lot of different drugs over a long period of time, and how do you deal with that?
应对手头的药物需求可能非常困难，但我认为如果你患有双相情感障碍，服药是必要的。对我来说，知道有药物可以帮助我，也减轻了污名感。这感觉像是，好吧，我的大脑里确实有些明确的问题是可以修复的。当然，药物对我产生了巨大的帮助，尽管我想补充关于抗精神病药物的一点，对于外行人来说，这是一个可怕的词，因为我从未精神错乱过。但事实上，事实证明是一种抗精神病药——阿立哌唑——对我帮助最大。所以你只需要对医生的建议保持开放的心态。治疗对我也至关重要。

23
00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:54.000
It can be very difficult to deal with the medication requirement, but I think it is necessary to be on medication if you're bipolar. It also, for me, felt less stigmatic to know that there was a medication that could help me. It felt like, okay, there was something definitively wrong in my brain that could be fixed. And certainly I've been helped tremendously by medication, although I'd like to add about antipsychotics that that's a terrifying word for a lay person to hear because I was never psychotic. But in fact, it turned out to be an antipsychotic ericip resolved. It helped me the most. So you just have to be open to your doctor's advice. Therapy has been essential for me.
我接受了30年的治疗，包括团体治疗、常规治疗，以及米克洛维茨博士提到的所有治疗。我都做过。我想我现在正在发现的——而且我现在做得很好——正念是我的康复中非常重要的一部分。仅仅是学会试着活在当下，不要有太多的判断，不对未来感到焦虑。这确实很有帮助。这是一种越来越普遍的治疗方法吗，米克洛维茨博士？是的，确实如此。我们对此做过一些研究，其他人也做过。正念，即正念认知疗法，已被发现对重度抑郁症有效，并且能。

24
00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:39.000
I've been in therapy for 30 years, group therapy, regular therapy, everything that Dr. Miklowitz mentioned. I've done it all. And I think what I'm discovering now and I'm doing very well now is mindfulness is a huge part of my recovery. Just learning to try to stay in the moment without too much judgment, without anxiety about the future. It really has been helpful. Is that a treatment that's becoming more common, Dr. McCluret? Yes, it is. We've done some studies on it, and others have as well. It's mindfulness, mindfulness-based cognitive therapy has been found to be effective for major depression and
预防重度抑郁症复发。有一些初步证据表明它对双相情感障碍也可能有效。目前尚不清楚它是否像预防抑郁那样有效地预防躁狂。实际上，这正是我要问你的，特里，你是否觉得正念在躁狂阶段也有帮助，它是否能很好地帮助你从高涨状态中平静下来？我对这个有一个有趣的反应。我其实觉得正念让我快乐得多。有时我认为我是轻躁狂，这是躁狂之下的状态，你感觉极好，生活非常顺利，你觉得自己处于最佳状态。所以我非常好奇，米克洛维茨博士，你是否听说过正念导致轻躁狂，

25
00:16:39.000 --> 00:17:27.000
preventing recurrences of major depression. There's some beginning evidence that it might be effective for bipolar disorder as well. It's not clear that it prevents mania as well as it prevents depression. That's actually what I was going to ask you, Terry, is whether or not you feel like the mindfulness helps during the manic phase as well, whether it helps bring you down from a high well. I have an interesting response to that. I think actually that mindfulness makes me so much happier. Sometimes I think I'm hypomanic, which is the state just below mania, where you're feeling terrific and life is going really well and you feel on top of your game. So I'm very curious, Dr. McLewitz, if you ever heard of mindfulness causing hypomania,
还是这只是我正在经历的一个极好的副作用？我没有看到任何相关数据，但我听别人说过。我听说过参加过小组的人说这给了他们特殊的洞察力，他们对这些洞察力感到非常兴奋。所以我认为就像任何抗抑郁药一样，也许它就像抗抑郁药物。当你改善情绪时，你也会面临将某人推入躁狂状态的风险。但没有相关数据。其他生活方式因素呢，比如保持规律睡眠或避免饮酒？

26
00:17:27.000 --> 00:18:02.000
or is that just a great side effect I'm experiencing? I haven't seen any data on it, but I've heard people say it. I've heard people who've been in the groups say that it gives them special insights, and they get very sort of excited about those insights. So I think like any antidepressant, maybe it's like antidepressant medication. When you improve mood, you also have the risk of setting someone into mania. But there's no date on it. What about other lifestyle factors such as getting regular sleep or avoiding alcohol?
特里，你谈到了避免饮酒对你来说有多重要。还有其他你觉得真的需要避免的诱惑吗？是的，我已经戒酒23年了，直到我停止饮酒后，我的药物才真正开始起作用。这对我来说是个彻底的打击，因为我以为不喝酒和同时服用这种药物只是一种建议。事实证明，我的大脑化学反应确实需要没有酒精来干扰药物。但至于其他生活方式因素，睡眠是至关重要的。对于患有双相情感障碍的我来说，睡眠一直是一件困难的事情。但有趣的是，我发现当我经历更年期时，当我开始使用荷尔蒙。

27
00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:54.000
Terry, you've talked about how important it is for you to avoid alcohol. Are there other triggers that you feel you really need to avoid? Yes, I've been sober for 23 years, and it wasn't until I stopped drinking that my medications actually began to work. And that was a total shock to me because I thought it was just a suggestion that you not drink alcohol and take this drug at the same time. It turned out to be that my brain chemistry just really needed to not have the alcohol interfering with the medication. But as far as other lifestyle factors, sleep is critical. Sleep has always been a difficult thing for me with bipolar disorder. but interestingly enough, I found when I went through menopause that when I got on hormonal
药物后，我的睡眠改善了，我的双相情感障碍也有所改善。所以这是一件需要检查的事情。还有，甲状腺——检查我的甲状腺也很重要，这会影响我的双相情感障碍。所以所有这些因素都是相互关联的。如果你想在精神上保持健康，我认为你需要过健康的生活方式。我能对此补充一点吗？可以，如果你愿意的话，请说。是的。我同意特里所说的一切，我认为她关于酒精所说的一切也可以。

28
00:18:54.000 --> 00:19:27.000
medications, my sleep improved and my bipolar disorder improved as well. So it's a thing to check out. Also, thyroid has, checking on my thyroid has been important that can impact my bipolar disorder. So there are all these interrelated factors. You need to live a healthy lifestyle. If you want to want to be healthy mentally, I think. Can I add to that as well? Yeah, if you would, yeah. Yeah. I agree with everything Terry said, and I think everything she said about alcohol could also
适用于大麻。有些人持续吸大麻，他们认为这是一种心境稳定剂，但实际上它以其独特的方式助长了你的情绪循环。如果没别的，它会干扰睡眠，有些人嗨了之后就睡不着觉，或者这会让他们在白天感到疲劳，所以他们会小睡。当你同时服用精神科药物时，任何滥用物质都不是一件好事，原因有多种。我们向双相情感障碍患者推荐的首要事情之一，就是尝试保持规律的睡眠-觉醒周期，这说起来容易，但实际上非常非常困难。

29
00:19:27.000 --> 00:20:11.000
apply to marijuana. There are people who smoke weed consistently, and they're thinking it's a mood stabilizer, but actually it can, in its own way, contribute to your mood cycling. If nothing else, by interfering with sleep, some people get high and then they can't fall asleep or it makes them sort of fatigue during the day so they take naps. There are various reasons why any substance of abuse is not a good thing to do when you're also taking psychiatric medications. One of the first things we recommend to people with bipolar disorders to try to stay on a regular sleep wake cycle, which sounds easy when you say it, but it actually is very, very difficult
因为首先这种疾病会干扰睡眠，当你患有这种疾病时很难入睡，而且你可能是轻躁狂，你的大脑可能会运转很快，或者你可能会感到抑郁，发现如果没有小睡很难熬过这一天。因此，首要策略之一就是试图让人们把就寝时间和起床时间控制在一定范围内，这样他们就不会睡过头，或者，你知道的，我们称之为“睡眠暴饮暴食”。现在有一种观点认为，双相情感障碍，特别是双相情感障碍的躁狂阶段，与创造力甚至创造天才有关。这有什么真实性吗，还是这只是一种神话？

30
00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:56.000
because first the disorder interferes with sleep and it's hard to get to sleep when you have the disorder and also you may be hypomanic, your mind may be going fast or you may be depressed and find it hard to get through the day without having a nap. So one of the first strategies is to try to get people to have their bedtime within a certain range and their wake-up time within a certain range so they don't oversleep or, you know, sleep binge, as we call it. Now, there's an idea that bipolar disorder, particularly the manic phase of bipolar disorder, is linked to creativity and even creative genius. Is there any truth to that, or is that just a myth?
你想回答这个吗，特里？我想插话并说，我认为绝对存在一种联系。在我的出版生涯中，我认识了许多许多双相情感障碍患者。在我心中，艺术与双相情感障碍普遍存在某种联系，不一定非得是躁狂。但当你患有双相情感障碍时，会发生一些事情。当你是一个艺术家时，它们会给你带来额外的优势。

31
00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:30.000
Do you want to answer that, Terry? I'd like to jump in and say, I think there's an absolute connection. I have known many, many bipolar people. over the course of my publishing career. And certainly there is a connection in my mind to artistry and just bipolar disorder in general, not necessarily mania. But there are things that happen when you're bipolar. They give you an extra edge when you're an artist.
因为你遭受了如此多的痛苦，所以你培养了极大的同理心。你可以把这一点带入你的艺术中。我认为当你患有双相情感障碍时，你在某种程度上也是一个局外人。这能让你观察人们，然后，你知道的，试图找出什么是正常行为。这也有助于创造力。所以我认为患有双相情感障碍的很多方面都与创造力息息相关。是的，我也赞同这一点。

32
00:21:30.000 --> 00:22:02.000
You develop a great deal of empathy because you've suffered so much. And so you can bring that to your art. I think you also are somewhat of an outsider when you're bipolar. And that allows you to watch people and, you know, trying to find out what's a normal behavior. That also helps with creativity. So I think there's a lot of that being bipolar that is connected to creativity. Yes. And I would agree with that.
当然有许多历史人物患有双相情感障碍。至少当我们回看时，他们看起来确实患有双相情感障碍。各种画家、古典音乐家柴可夫斯基、贝多芬，我们认为可能都患有双相情感障碍。库尔特·柯本（Kurt Cobain），更近一些，可能也患有双相情感障碍。这种联系——尽管我们认为人们可能在轻躁狂期间比在躁狂期间创造更多。

33
00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:42.000
Certainly there is many. historical figures who've had bipolar disorder. At least when we look back, it certainly looks like they had bipolar disorder. Various painters, Chikowsky, the classic musician, the Beethoven, we think, probably had bipolar disorder. Kurt Cobain, more recently, had probably had bipolar disorder. And the linkage, though we think it may be that people create more during hypomania than mania,
因为这确实能帮你稍微加速，你的想法运转得更快，当你处于轻躁狂状态时，你会更有跳出常规思考的倾向。但当你陷入躁狂时，我不知道特里是否同意这一点，但我认为人们会产出很多作品，但那并不是他们最好的作品。绝对的，我同意这一点。当我处于轻躁狂时，我能看到事物之间的联系。这太惊人了。

34
00:22:42.000 --> 00:23:06.000
because it certainly helps you to speed up a little bit, and your thoughts go faster, and you kind of think outside the box a little bit more when you're hypomanic. But when you get manic, I don't know if Terry would agree with this, but I think people produce a lot of work, but it's not their best work. Absolutely, I'd agree with that. When I'm hypomanic, I see connections between things. It's astonishing.
就像宇宙的结构似乎都连在了一起，这对我有意义。我能够对此进行写作。但当我处于躁狂时，我认为自己有了所有这些美妙的、宏大的想法，我把它们写在纸上。通常是用我能想象到的最微小的字体写出来的，等我不躁狂时，我就无法破译了。所以我大部分躁狂时的写作都付诸东流了。所以，调整药物以便能够保持在轻躁狂水平，因为它是有效的，这基本上是一个目标吗？这是凯·贾姆森（Kay Jamison）写过很多的一个话题。她是一位心理学家，也是一位作家。

35
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:52.000
It's like the fabric of the universe just seems to be connected and it makes sense to me. And I'm able to write about that. But when I'm manic, I think I have all these fabulous, grandiose ideas, and I put them down on paper. And it's usually in the tiniest imaginable little script that I can't decipher later when I'm not manic. So most of my manic writing has gone out the window. So is it a goal then to basically titrate the medicine so that you can sort of keep at that hypo level because it's effective? That's something that Kay Jameson has written a lot about. She was a psychologist and also a writer.
她谈到了寻找锂盐的最佳水平，这使她能够保留一些轻躁狂，同时仍能防止复发。这是一个平衡的艺术。对不同的人来说是不同的。你必须让你的医生认同拥有，你知道的，“我能服用较低水平的锂盐吗？”但对于一些艺术家来说，能够取得这种平衡是至关重要的。特里，我看到你写过关于你如何认为你的双相情感障碍导致你的行动非常受限，尤其是当你感到抑郁的时候。你能谈谈那个吗？比如，那是什么感觉？

36
00:23:52.000 --> 00:24:29.000
She talked about finding the sort of optimal level of lithium that allowed her some hypomania but still protected against recurrences. And that is a balancing act. It's different for different people. You have to get your doctor on board with having, you know, can I take a lower lithium level? But for some people who are artists, that's very critical to be able to make that balance. Terry, I saw that you wrote about how you believe your bipolar disorder has led you to being very circumscribed in your movements, especially when you're feeling depressed. Can you talk about that? Like, what is that like?
你是如何发现自己在那个方面有些不同的？哦，金，我很高兴你提出这一点，因为这是我经常撰写的主题，而且我收到了关于这个主题最多的回应，因为我认为许多人正遭受着所谓的精神运动性迟缓，即抑郁状态下身体精神和身体过程的减慢。这太可怕了。这就像一种瘫痪。如果我，我正坐在我的桌子前，我想伸出手去拿我的笔，我都做不到。

37
00:24:29.000 --> 00:25:07.000
And how did you discover that you were sort of different in that respect? Oh, Kim, I'm so glad you brought that up because that's something that I have written about frequently, and I've gotten the most responses to that subject because I think many people are suffering from what is called psychomotor retardation, a slowing down of the mental and physical processes of the body in depression. And it is terrifying. It's like a paralysis. If I were to, I'm sitting at my desk right now and I want to reach out and grab my pen, I couldn't do that.
我必须思考、思考、再思考。我的手仍然不听大脑的指挥。这就是精神运动性迟缓。我认为它确实没有得到它应该获得的关注，因为精神兴奋剂在这方面对我有所帮助，我认为其他人也可以从中受益，米克洛维茨博士可以对此进行探讨，但对我来说，这真的是患有双相情感障碍最糟糕的部分。是的，我实际上在想——我之前要问你这个，特里，这是否延伸到了早晨起床？你发现当你抑郁时，甚至连起床都非常困难吗？

38
00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:53.000
I'd have to think about it and think about it and ponder it. And my hands still would not obey my mind. That's psychomotor retardation. And I think it really does not get the amount of attention that it should because I have been helped by psychostimulants with that and I think other people could be as well and Dr. Micklowitz could address that but it really is for me the worst part of being bipolar. Yeah and I wonder actually I was going to ask you this earlier Terry does that extend to the to getting out of bed in the morning do you find that when you're depressed it's very difficult to even move out of bed?
我抑郁时就生活在我的床上。我不怎么去除了厨房或浴室以外的地方，但不幸的是，床成了我的宇宙，因为你根本无法把，你无法掀开被子。它太重了。你有没有为这个特定问题找到任何有效的治疗方法？当它是非常严重的抑郁时，除了时间什么都帮不上忙。但当它没那么严重时，我再次发现精神兴奋剂是有帮助的，比如莫达非尼对我很有帮助。

39
00:25:53.000 --> 00:26:34.000
I live in my bed when I'm depressed. I don't go much. further than the kitchen or the bathroom, but the bed becomes my universe, unfortunately, because you simply can't get the, you can't get the comforter off. It's too heavy. Have you found any effective treatments for that particular problem? When it's really severe depression, nothing helps but time. But when it's not quite as bad, again, I found that psychostimulants have been helpful, like modafinil helps me.
有时利他林（Ritalin）会有所帮助，或者所有这些药物的组合，连同我的心境稳定剂和我的抗抑郁药。所以我通过药物找到了一些帮助，但我希望有人能帮助我克服它会复发的恐惧，因为，再次强调，这是患有双相情感障碍最糟糕的部分。是的，我认为家人们最挣扎的事情之一就是他们会说：“好吧，如果你只是起床，你就会没事了。如果你只是，你知道的，强迫自己做事情，你会快乐得多。”然而他们不明白的是，当你带着双相情感障碍醒来时，就像胸口压了一百磅重的石头。

40
00:26:34.000 --> 00:27:21.000
Sometimes Ritalin can be helpful or a combination of all of those and along with my mood stabilizers and my antidepressants. So I have found some help with the medication, but I wish someone could help with the terror of it returning because, again, it is the worst part of being bipolar. Yeah, and I think one of the things families struggle with most is they say, well, if you would just get out of bed, you'd be fine. And if you would just, you know, kind of make yourself do things, you know, you'll be a lot happier. Yet what they don't get is that when you wake up with bipolar disorder, it's like having a hundred pound weight on your chest.
你动不了。即使你愿意，即使你觉得你应该这么做，这也是一种很难克服的生理状态。家庭成员很难理解这一点。他们会说：“嗯，我今天早上起床了。你怎么就起不来？”但这是一个由抑郁症引起的严重的生理局限。这让我回到了以家庭为中心的治疗。我的意思是，当你做这个时，你用的一些技巧是什么？因为当爸爸妈妈说“孩子，快起床吧”，而孩子却起不来时，这肯定是患者听起来非常难受的话。所以，我的意思是，你如何引导家庭度过。

41
